 ## Overview
-Training Ray Compositing (Sigma) composites ray samples (RGB and sigma) into final image colors, alpha weights, and depth using volume rendering, specifically designed to support gradient computation for training [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard inference compositing by explicitly computing gradients for density (`sigmas`), color (`rgbs`), and ambient light contributions, enabling end-to-end optimization of the underlying neural fields [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Training Ray Compositing](3785-training-ray-compositing.md) by providing the differentiable forward pass required for backpropagation.
+Training Ray Compositing (Sigma) is a differentiable volume rendering technique that composites per-ray samples of density ($\sigma$), color ($\text{RGB}$), and ambient light into final pixel values while computing gradients for all inputs during the backward pass [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard inference compositing by explicitly supporting gradient propagation to density and color parameters, enabling end-to-end training of neural radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Training Ray Compositing](3785-training-ray-compositing.md) by providing the specific implementation for training with sigma and ambient contributions.
 
 ## How it works
-The method performs differentiable alpha compositing along rays. It accumulates transparency and weighted color/depth contributions while simultaneously computing gradients for the input density and color fields.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+The method takes pre-sampled ray data and produces composite image colors, accumulated alpha weights, and depth values, along with gradients for the inputs.
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
